  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 341 PM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\nUpper level ridging will gradually build into the region Friday and \nSaturday of Labor Day weekend. This will bring very warm \ntemperatures in the low to mid 90s, with upper 90s southwest. \nAtmospheric moisture from New Mexico and Colorado will increase into \nthe region with south southwesterly flow aloft. Low chances for \nshowers/thunderstorms southwest and east Friday night, with low \nchances across much of the area Saturday night. By Sunday and \nMonday, the upper ridge may begin to flatten out as an upper trough \nmoves front the Intermountain west into the Northern Plains. Highs \nremain around 10 degrees above normal in the upper 80s to lower 90s. \nPWATs will increase some during this time with chance POPs Sunday \nnight and again Monday night. NBM 24 hours probability of exceeding \n0.25 inch ending 12Z Monday and again 12Z Tuesday, ranges from 20 to \n40 percent. \n\nThe upper trough is  forecast to flatten out by next Wednesday as an \nupper trough moves into the Great Lakes. This may bring some relief \nfrom the heat with highs from 80 to around 85.",
